Inventory every ordinary backend-to-TUI state transfer and every action result carrying replacement state. Start with all fields and callback signatures of TuiRuntimeOptions, TuiRuntimeStateSnapshot, TuiSubmitResult and reload results, then follow app/tui/interactive_tui.cpp initial setup/state_snapshot construction and every consumer in runtime_state.*, runtime_views.*, selectors and rendering. Include session metadata, modes, context counts, completion catalogs/generations, settings, themes, trust, startup overview, todos, model/session/user-turn selectors and any backend-created SelectListView or equivalent presentation object. Search producers and call sites as well as names: a structure without Snapshot in its name can still be the obsolete transport.

For each field or coherent group, record the authoritative backend owner and mutation sites; initial and subsequent delivery paths; frontend consumers; current formatting owner; unknown/loading/empty/error semantics; copying cost and refresh frequency; generation rules; and proposed canonical-data domain and TuiFrontEnd projection. Identify state duplicated between startup options, returned snapshots, callbacks and other caches. Separate immutable construction dependencies and actual action outcomes from observable state piggybacked on commands. An error, action identifier or quit/control result is not itself replacement presentation state; document the exact distinction for each callback rather than treating every callback as forbidden.

Produce a state-transfer inventory artifact with a contents entry and source references. Every discovered path must map to a later migration goal, a concrete removal target and existing or needed regression coverage. Proposals must preserve backend ownership and frontend formatting without simply wrapping TuiRuntimeStateSnapshot wholesale or renaming transported views. Flag overlaps with transition and interactive-protocol audits for their resolution; do not implement migration code. Acceptance requires explicit coverage of all runtime options and state-bearing action returns, including backend implementation types/includes exposed through their signatures, with no unexplained exclusions.
